@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+# Bounded scroll-continuity probe (synthetic, not installed-client acceptance)
+
+Run from the repository root with existing Desktop workspace dependencies and the matching Playwright headless Chromium already prepared:
+
+```sh
+node contrib/scroll-continuity/browser.mjs /absolute/task-owned/evidence
+```
+
+This uses the **unmodified official Vite configuration**, production Thread/Markdown/CSS, native ResizeObserver/IntersectionObserver and scroll owner, real `reconcileAuthoritativeChatMessages` and `toRuntimeMessage`. The HTML entry is `apps/desktop/scroll-probe.html`. No backend starts, no model is called, and no private conversation/config/database is read. The plain-text snapshot and delayed history-page producer are explicitly synthetic. This does not emulate wire reconnect/ordering or prove Electron/Windows adoption.
+
+Seventeen stable case IDs cover bottom growth, genuine wheel escape, reading anchors, repeated real source reconciliation with durable identities, completion, same-key empty/reload restoration, asynchronous Show-earlier prepend, variable row height after the reader takes ownership, explicit jump/resumed following, a render-budgeted next-turn control, and successive content-resize observer samples reaching the bottom before the next frame. The history button is invoked through its DOM click handler without Playwright scrolling it into view, so the test does not confuse deliberate navigation to the button with preservation of the prior reading anchor.
+
+`frames.json` contains rAF and content-ResizeObserver samples (distinguished by `phase`) with viewport/scrollTop/scrollHeight, following state and on-screen logical rowId/renderer-id/geometry. The observer tracks **outer groups**; it never asks skipped off-screen message descendants for layout. Samples are diagnostic, not proof that every sample was painted. `results.json` contains exact case outcomes and settled snapshots. Raw screenshots are 1440x960. Browser and Vite exit in `finally` on success and failure.
+
+The cold same-key reload first mounts a bounded tail and backfills. Its oracle waits for the remembered logical anchor to become reachable (10-second failure bound), rather than interpreting a fixed one-second in-progress snapshot as settled failure. It does **not** claim zero transient loading displacement. Existing post-restore frozen-distance behavior intentionally preserves from-bottom through late layout until real user input; a genuine wheel gesture precedes the independent variable-height oracle.
+
+The repair is deliberately local: only the content ResizeObserver advances its previous-size baseline. A delayed scroll event caused by the previous bottom pin must not consume the next DOM growth before that growth's ResizeObserver. A natural baseline trace exposed this ordering, while another natural retry did not (it is timing-dependent). The browser fixture therefore has a controlled actor: it appends real text, then delivers one synthetic pending `scroll` from a MutationObserver after that DOM text changes and before the native ResizeObserver. It checks that this actor actually delivered once; no scrollHeight, clientHeight, layout, resize callback or model/backend output is fabricated. The same final browser case is red on baseline and green on the candidate.
+
+`list-stream-resize-order.test.tsx` independently makes that ordering deterministic with the real Thread component under the existing jsdom harness; its geometry/RO are explicit stubs, unlike the browser. It also asserts that genuine wheel escape still wins. The production fix removes one height-sampling assignment from the scroll callback, adds no new owner/state/ordering protocol, and leaves the existing library follow path intact. Its live observer is re-created in a layout effect on session/profile storage-owner changes and empty-to-loaded content: a baseline from a shorter outgoing transcript must not mistake the restored incoming running transcript for stream growth. Rebinding during the commit prevents a native RO from running against an old owner's dimensions before passive effects.
+
+Three additional `owner-switch-*` IDs are a separate cohort, not extra executions of the original seventeen. They use the same mounted viewport/content and a real running-state marker, with synthetic four-row minimum heights of 500px in A and 1250px in B to make incoming geometry available at commit time. This explicit fixture CSS does not claim organic Markdown/backfill timing coverage. A is already running before switching to running B; the colocated `list-session-resize-owner.test.tsx` separately waits for real runtime publication in the idle-A to running-B case. B has a remembered 800px reading gap. A MutationObserver watching the committed owner attribute delivers one synthetic pending scroll before native RO, and the native-RO samples must retain 800px. `owner-frames.json` records same viewport/content identity, actual running state, content height and before/after geometry; dimensions and resize callbacks remain native. On the first candidate without owner rebinding this case is red (800px becomes 0), while the revised candidate stays at 800px. This fixture's fixed-row whitespace is intentional test geometry, not production UI styling.
+
+The inherited commentary-reconciliation patch is separate provenance, not authored by this probe. The original user occurrence and installed Windows client still need their existing QA owner/window; this is not proof of adoption or unique incident attribution.
